WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:50.777
[Music]

00:00:50.777 --> 00:01:00.147
[Narrator:] Sanitation is the key to permanent rat control, for it strikes at the rat's basic requirements of food and harborage.

00:01:00.147 --> 00:01:08.227
To reinforce the effects of sanitation, rats must be cut off from entry into buildings through any actual or potential openings.

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:18.740
An exposed wooden edge on a door affords the rat a starting place for gnawing and eventual entry.

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:30.400
Starting with a one-eighth inch crack, a rat can gnaw an opening in a single night.

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:37.440
Wooden window frames are penetrated with the same ease.

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:43.100
Most foundation vents are open doors for rats.

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:50.700
Service wires can be runways to entrances otherwise beyond reach.

00:01:54.340 --> 00:02:00.220
Ratproofing is the treatment of buildings to prevent rat entry or exit.

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:11.040
The gnawing ability of rats is remarkable.

00:02:11.047 --> 00:02:15.207
Their cutting tools are their long, sharp incisors.

00:02:15.207 --> 00:02:21.507
In one series of experiments, rats in search of food cut through a variety of materials.

00:02:21.507 --> 00:02:29.047
One-eighth inch compressed fiber hardboard with a quarter-inch gnawing edge was penetrated in two nights.

00:02:29.047 --> 00:02:33.207
Quarter-inch plywood with no gnawing edge, four nights.

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:40.300
Three-eighths inch plasterboard with no gnawing edge, five nights.

00:02:40.307 --> 00:02:46.607
Three-eighths inch marine sheathing, no gnawing edge, seven nights.

00:02:46.607 --> 00:02:52.277
Two-inch foam glass, no gnawing edge, 13 nights.

00:02:52.277 --> 00:03:02.007
Twenty-four SO aluminum alloy almost a tenth of an inch thick, gnawing edge one-quarter inch, 11 nights.

00:03:02.007 --> 00:03:11.377
Asbestos flexboard three-sixteenth's inch thick with an eleven-sixteenths inch hole took 51 nights.

00:03:11.377 --> 00:03:18.547
With a simulated channel of 61 ST aluminum alloy, one-fiftieth of an inch thick with a quarter-inch gnawing edge,

00:03:18.547 --> 00:03:24.607
it took 170 nights to penetrate one thickness.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:50.700
Some materials, however, such as galvanized sheet iron, are almost completely rat-resistant.

00:03:50.707 --> 00:03:58.467
Galvanized sheet iron and galvanized hardware cloth are the basic materials used in typical ratproofing operations.

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:10.300
Large centralized workshops should be equipped with a metal slither, a specially designed metal-bound work table,

00:04:10.307 --> 00:04:19.587
templates developed for use in repetitive shop operations, and a complete set of sheet metal hand tools.

00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:39.377
A metal-bending brake is also essential.

00:04:39.377 --> 00:04:45.947
Employees should be skilled sheet metal workers, since each item fabricated must be custom-made.

00:04:45.947 --> 00:04:53.087
Careful attention to field specifications is necessary, or the completed work will be useless and will have to be scrapped.

00:04:55.600 --> 00:05:00.800
Central workshops speed ratproofing operations by providing adequate workspace

00:05:00.807 --> 00:05:05.307
for the required equipment, much of which is large and cumbersome.

00:05:05.307 --> 00:05:12.977
Close attention should be given to the arrangement of the equipment, so as to save time in moving materials from one to the other.

00:05:12.977 --> 00:05:18.807
A complete array of sheet metal equipment expedites fabrication, provides consistent quality,

00:05:18.807 --> 00:05:26.747
and gives the finished materials a uniform craftsman-like appearance which is pleasing to the user and to the public.

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:33.500
Fabrication instructions must be based upon efficient design.

00:05:33.507 --> 00:05:37.427
They must specify proper materials and methods of assembly,

00:05:37.660 --> 00:05:43.900
so as to assure adequate protection against rat gnawing over a maximum life span.

00:05:44.060 --> 00:05:48.840
Thus, galvanized hardware cloth could be used without a metal frame,

00:05:48.847 --> 00:05:55.867
but experience has shown that its useful life is greatly prolonged by placing it in a sturdy metal frame.

00:05:58.120 --> 00:06:10.140
Large items can be fabricated easily in the shop, thus field personnel are freed to devote their time to installation work and to fabricating small items.

00:06:10.147 --> 00:06:18.707
In order to expedite installation of ratproofing materials on buildings, a mobile workshop can be designed to permit simple, on-the-job fabrication

00:06:19.180 --> 00:06:22.400
and to carry equipment necessary for installation.

00:06:22.540 --> 00:06:31.877
It should provide work tables, a metal-bending brake, and hand tools for metalwork, woodwork, and masonry.

00:06:31.877 --> 00:06:40.147
Also, it must have storage space for standard ratproofing materials such as galvanized sheet metal, galvanized hardware cloth,

00:06:40.147 --> 00:06:44.947
cement, bricks, and for various sizes of lumber.

00:06:44.947 --> 00:06:50.267
The materials used and the method of ratproofing depend on the job to be done.

00:06:53.320 --> 00:06:58.640
This wooden door was cut through because its loose fit gave the rat a gnawing edge.

00:06:58.647 --> 00:07:03.507
It can be ratproofed with a standard metal cuff and channel.

00:07:03.507 --> 00:07:08.647
The threshold should be of metal or at least should be covered with sheet metal.

00:07:08.647 --> 00:07:13.207
Clearances around the door should be three-eighths inch or less.

00:07:13.207 --> 00:07:20.067
Treatment of the door, the threshold, and the jambs with sheet metal prevents rat entry by gnawing.

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:28.940
Naturally, if the door is left open, it fails to keep out the rats.

00:07:28.947 --> 00:07:33.547
More rats enter buildings through open doors than by any other means.

00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:45.040
If a door must be kept open for ventilation or other purposes,

00:07:45.047 --> 00:07:51.647
the entrance should be protected by a rat-proof screen door equipped with self-closing hinges.

00:07:51.647 --> 00:07:55.087
The lower edge of the screen door should be cuffed and channeled.

00:08:02.180 --> 00:08:08.980
The rats cannot enter any opening if the width or height of the opening is less than one-half inch.

00:08:19.477 --> 00:08:23.747
Hardware cloth with a half-inch mesh will keep rats out.

00:08:23.747 --> 00:08:27.127
A quarter-inch mesh will keep mice out also.

00:08:28.520 --> 00:08:33.140
This window should be repaired.

00:08:33.147 --> 00:08:42.787
A ratproofing screen of a quarter or half-inch mesh hardware cloth in a sheet metal frame should be installed in front of the window.

00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:59.200
Foundation vents should be protected by hardware cloth of a quarter or a half-inch mesh.

00:09:01.840 --> 00:09:09.000
Holes left by the installation of service pipes should be filled immediately with a good grade of cement mortar.

00:09:09.007 --> 00:09:15.577
Such ratproofing measures, while of the utmost importance, are relatively simply and obvious.

00:09:15.577 --> 00:09:19.247
Others are more difficult and extensive.

00:09:19.247 --> 00:09:22.447
For instance, this cellar is rat-infested.

00:09:22.447 --> 00:09:24.287
How did rats get in?

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:32.340
They burrowed under the foundation wall and entered through the earthen floor of the cellar.

00:09:35.920 --> 00:09:40.600
Ratproofing of cellars can be accomplished in one of two ways.

00:09:40.607 --> 00:09:49.987
An L-shaped curtain wall of concrete can be built adjoining the foundation wall to a depth of 24 inches below the surface of the ground.

00:09:50.420 --> 00:09:56.340
The lip of the curtain wall should be at least 12 inches wide and four inches thick.

00:09:56.840 --> 00:10:01.077
Curtain walls are effective because of the habits of rats.

00:10:01.077 --> 00:10:07.877
Occasionally, rats seeking an entry may burrow eight to 12 feet deep beside a foundation wall.

00:10:07.877 --> 00:10:13.947
Usually they will not give up unless they meet a horizontal obstruction, which they take to be a floor.

00:10:13.947 --> 00:10:18.447
Rats apparently refuse to burrow away from their objective.

00:10:18.447 --> 00:10:24.647
L-shaped curtain walls of these dimensions have thus far baffled rats.

00:10:24.647 --> 00:10:31.147
Another way of ratproofing a cellar is by installing a concrete floor closely joined to the foundation wall.

00:10:35.180 --> 00:10:41.400
This is the method of choice because it excludes rats, and at the same time adds value to the building.

00:10:43.547 --> 00:10:50.277
Rats excluded from a building by sealing off their usual routes will seek other ways of access,

00:10:50.277 --> 00:10:55.847
thus it is necessary to make it impossible for rats to enter less-accessible points.

00:10:55.847 --> 00:11:01.247
For example, either this tree should come down or the window should be rat-proofed.

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:06.100
These eaves are potential points of entry.

00:11:06.107 --> 00:11:10.767
They are accessible from the roof and from the wires along the side of the house.

00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:20.977
Covering the eaves with hardware cloth protects the building from rats and also from squirrels and birds.

00:11:20.977 --> 00:11:25.217
Ventilation, the purpose of the open eaves, remains effective.

00:11:27.320 --> 00:11:32.240
Roofs, which rats are able to reach, need special attention.

00:11:32.247 --> 00:11:41.807
On this roof, rats are harboring at the junction of the parapet wall and the roof, and are entering the building through the soil vent pipe.

00:11:41.807 --> 00:11:45.947
Hardware cloth protects the soil vent from rat entry.

00:11:45.947 --> 00:11:50.707
The next step should be to eliminate the harborage at the parapet wall.

00:11:50.707 --> 00:11:57.067
In any building, every probable point of rat entry must be located and then ratproofed.

00:11:58.200 --> 00:12:01.977
There are many types of buildings that need ratproofing.

00:12:01.977 --> 00:12:11.007
Each has problems peculiar to its geographical location, the type of construction, and proximity to other buildings.

00:12:11.007 --> 00:12:14.587
But it is not only the exteriors of buildings that have to be treated.

00:12:15.780 --> 00:12:23.777
Many establishments are so constructed that they furnish an unnecessary amount of interior structural harborage.

00:12:23.777 --> 00:12:27.897
The double-partition wall is the most common example of structural harborage.

00:12:29.120 --> 00:12:34.900
Within this double wall, for instance, there is a Norway rat nest.

00:12:34.907 --> 00:12:38.387
Another double wall provides a perfect runway for roof rats.

00:12:40.660 --> 00:12:47.000
After ratproofing is completed, rats usually are found to be still in the building.

00:12:47.007 --> 00:12:51.977
Where a building contains sources of food and water plus adequate harborage,

00:12:51.977 --> 00:12:58.077
the rat population inside the building will continue to thrive until further rat-control measures are taken.

00:13:01.920 --> 00:13:06.700
The remaining rats must be killed.

00:13:06.707 --> 00:13:14.407
Special rat-killing techniques have been devised to speed rat eradication in ratproof buildings.

00:13:14.407 --> 00:13:19.007
These special techniques should be used immediately after ratproofing.

00:13:22.520 --> 00:13:28.500
In urban areas, ratproofing problems usually involve adjoining buildings.

00:13:28.507 --> 00:13:37.507
Where buildings have exterior walls almost adjoining, rats may harbor between the walls, feeding in the adjacent premises.

00:13:45.640 --> 00:13:52.480
Or where the buildings have a common wall, the rats may harbor in one building and feed in the next.

00:13:58.180 --> 00:14:03.340
This fact has an important bearing on ratproofing of urban property.

00:14:03.347 --> 00:14:09.487
Here is a half of a city block with a frontage of 200 feet and a depth of 75 feet.

00:14:11.960 --> 00:14:16.320
This half-block is divided into eight premises of equal size.

00:14:17.977 --> 00:14:22.757
Each has a frontage of 25 feet and a depth of 75 feet.

00:14:24.560 --> 00:14:33.400
If each premise is to be ratproofed separately, work will have to be done on a total of 1075 linear feet.

00:14:33.407 --> 00:14:40.807
Such ratproofing is expensive, but it is necessary to ratproof each premise separately.

00:14:40.807 --> 00:14:45.907
Study of rat habits indicates that rats live in a limited home range.

00:14:45.907 --> 00:14:48.077
Rarely do they cross the street.

00:14:48.077 --> 00:14:56.617
Therefore, for economy in ratproofing, the entire half-block must be considered not as individual premises but as one unit.

00:14:57.877 --> 00:15:07.457
Ratproofing the perimeter of the whole group of adjoining premises ratproofs the entire half-block, and reduces the area to be treated to about half.

00:15:09.107 --> 00:15:18.827
Ratproofing on a block basis is both the most efficient and most economical procedure, and complies with the island theory of rat control.

00:15:19.807 --> 00:15:24.747
The island theory assumes that each block forms an island of rat life.

00:15:24.747 --> 00:15:30.007
After the block has been ratproofed, the rats within each premise are eradicated.

00:15:31.247 --> 00:15:36.977
After a block has been ratproofed and rat-free, it has to be maintained.

00:15:36.977 --> 00:15:42.117
Damage to rat-proof installations can occur through accidents or carelessness.

00:15:45.707 --> 00:15:48.567
Such damage may result in reinfestation.

00:15:59.007 --> 00:16:03.107
The window must be ratproofed again and the building freed of rats.

00:16:10.147 --> 00:16:20.547
The most efficient, the most economical, and the most lasting ratproofing results from proper design and construction of rat-proof buildings.

00:16:20.547 --> 00:16:25.907
Rat-proof materials are used at all rat-accessible points.

00:16:25.907 --> 00:16:35.547
Material having a maximum opening of one-half inch in the least dimension, is used at all potential points of entry that can be reached by rats.

00:16:35.547 --> 00:16:44.807
Expanded metal, as installed here, is far superior to hardware cloth, both in strength and permanence.

00:16:44.807 --> 00:16:51.477
Rat-proof construction eliminates most of the interior harborage found in usual construction.

00:16:51.477 --> 00:16:59.277
There are solid basement floors and no double floors, or ceilings.

00:16:59.277 --> 00:17:04.047
No opening is left around service pipes where they pass through walls.

00:17:04.047 --> 00:17:07.107
There are no double walls.

00:17:07.107 --> 00:17:14.907
There are no enclosed spaces in stairwells, nor boxed-in spaces in or under shelving.

00:17:14.907 --> 00:17:22.677
These are but a few of the features recommended by revised building codes aimed at the rat-proof construction of buildings.

00:17:22.677 --> 00:17:27.777
Buildings so constructed can be kept rat-free with a minimum of labor and expense.

00:17:29.000 --> 00:17:37.680
When we learn to practice continuous anti-rat sanitation, when all our buildings have been ratproofed by techniques such as these,

00:17:40.020 --> 00:17:53.077
followed by thorough rat eradication, then man will have gone far toward ridding himself of his unwanted and deadly companion, the rat.

00:17:53.077 --> 00:18:07.837
[Music]

